=== layout: section

# Controversial Biotech and Pharma Ethics

=== layout: subsection

# 40. Controversial Biotechnology

=== layout: bookend

# 40. Questions

@body

- Choose two biotechnology controversies the class has yet to discuss. What concerned people at the time? Whose views changed as evidence or experience accumulated?

@citation

Discussion prompt source: BIOL 480, Fall 2026 Set #7, p. 99.

=== layout: one-panel

# 40. Choose Two Cases and Compare Them

@body

- Name the technology and what it claims to do.
- Identify what was actually demonstrated and what people disagreed about.
- Ask whether the disagreement is about evidence, values, or who gets to decide.

@citation

[UNESCO, Universal Declaration on Bioethics and Human Rights](https://www.unesco.org/en/legal-affairs/universal-declaration-bioethics-and-human-rights)

=== layout: two-panels

# 40. A Common Format for Comparison

@left

*Case 1*

Who receives the benefit? Who bears the risk? Name one finding that would change your position.

@right

*Case 2*

Ask the same questions. Which disagreement between the two cases is about values?

@citation

[UNESCO, Universal Declaration on Bioethics and Human Rights](https://www.unesco.org/en/legal-affairs/universal-declaration-bioethics-and-human-rights)

=== layout: one-panel

# 40. Example for Comparison: Access to Naloxone

@body

In 2023 the FDA approved a naloxone nasal spray for sale without a prescription. Naloxone can reverse an opioid overdose.

- The regulatory change removed one barrier to obtaining this product.
- If a store never stocks it or the price is too high, what benefit will a neighbor actually see?

@citation

[FDA, First over-the-counter naloxone nasal spray (2023)](https://www.fda.gov/news-events/press-announcements/fda-approves-first-over-counter-naloxone-nasal-spray)

=== layout: one-panel

# 40. Example for Comparison: Access to Genome Data

@body

A research team wants to reuse HeLa genome data. Controlled access can help protect a family's privacy, but adds a step for researchers.

- Compare this with access to naloxone. What is being made accessible in each case?
- Use these as practice, then choose two different controversies for your own talking point.

@citation

[NIH, The NIH-Lacks Family Agreement](https://grants.nih.gov/policy-and-compliance/policy-topics/sharing-policies/dms/privacy/nih-lacks-family-agreement)

=== layout: one-panel

# 40. A 60-second Position You Could Defend

@body

Suppose a rural clinic can join a tissue bank, but the community has no seat on its review board.

- A resident might support sharing samples if local patients receive results they can use.
- Another resident might require a voice in deciding future uses. What evidence could resolve the first issue? What agreement could resolve the second?

@citation

[UNESCO, Universal Declaration on Bioethics and Human Rights](https://www.unesco.org/en/legal-affairs/universal-declaration-bioethics-and-human-rights)

=== layout: subsection

# 41. Other Pharmaceutical Ethics

=== layout: bookend

# 41. Questions

@body

- Select two other topics in pharmaceutical regulation or ethics that have not been discussed before in class and present them.

@citation

Discussion prompt source: BIOL 480, Fall 2026 Set #7, p. 101.

=== layout: one-panel

# 41. Pricing and Evidence Disclosure

@body

- A drug price can affect patient access even when a treatment works.
- Selective reporting can make a treatment look more useful or safer than the full evidence suggests.
- Distinguish a question about affordability from a question about reliable evidence.

@citation

[Turner et al., Selective publication of antidepressant trials (2008)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18199864/)  |  [KFF Health Tracking Poll, March 2026, question 7](https://files.kff.org/attachment/topline-kff-health-tracking-poll-march-2026.pdf)

=== layout: one-panel

# 41. Two Questions for the Company

@body

- What would show that patients can actually get the product?
- What study results are available for an independent review?
- Identify the groups that would be helped or harmed by each policy.

@citation

[Chan et al., Protocols compared with published trials (2004)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15161896/)  |  [KFF Health Tracking Poll, March 2026, question 7](https://files.kff.org/attachment/topline-kff-health-tracking-poll-march-2026.pdf)

=== layout: one-panel

# 41. Which Antidepressant Trials Reached Readers?

@body

Turner and colleagues compared the FDA record for 74 studies with the published literature. They found that 31% of the studies had gone unpublished.

- Someone reading only published papers would see a more favorable picture.
- How could a trial registry help a reader discover a study whose results never appeared in a journal?

@citation

[Turner et al., Selective publication of antidepressant trials (2008)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18199864/)

=== layout: big-image

@image

![How Does the Record Change the Picture?](assets/set7_drawings/publication_bias.drawn.svg)

@caption

Topic 41: Which Record Did the Reader See?\
How did selective publication change the apparent share of positive trials?

@citation

[Turner et al., Selective publication of antidepressant trials (2008)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18199864/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 41. When an Effective Medicine Stays at the Pharmacy

@body

In KFF's March 2026 U.S. poll, adults reported what they did in the past year because of cost.

- *27%* left a prescription unfilled. *19%* cut pills in half or skipped doses.
- One person could report both. Why would adding the percentages give a misleading estimate of how many people had trouble affording medicine?

@citation

[KFF Health Tracking Poll, March 2026, question 7](https://files.kff.org/attachment/topline-kff-health-tracking-poll-march-2026.pdf)

=== layout: one-panel

# 41. What Outcome Was Promised at the Start?

@body

Chan and colleagues compared clinical trial protocols with the papers that followed them. They found differences in which outcomes were reported.

- An outcome specified before results are known gives readers a reference point.
- If a paper highlights a favorable secondary outcome, where would you look for the original primary outcome?

@citation

[Chan et al., Protocols compared with published trials (2004)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15161896/)
